two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times 6=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(2,6),\\,(3,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(3,4)$ add to $7:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+4 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $3x+4x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+6 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+4x+6 & = \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+3x)+(4x+6) & = \\\\ x(2x+3)+2(2x+3) & = \\\\ (x+2)(2x+3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064748 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "842ce2e6-4b5f-56db-b8c0-d7317ee65b28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064748 #edb/ref 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{18}}{{z}^{7}} & = \\\\ {z}^{(18-7)} & = \\\\ {z}^{11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065071 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065071 #edb/ref 1061 165334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9adebaa0-c60d-5909-ab40-deaebd964a20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065072 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065072 #edb/ref 1061 165335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065072 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e3510aa7-0d8b-5b73-ba73-c850214d0a6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065072 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065073 #edb/ref 105