#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065195 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d4e0fa30-ed7c-5ef6-9aaa-10d6ba2f9c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065195 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065196 #edb/ref 1061 3530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57c38b87-4e15-573a-960c-a61055841b16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065197 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065197 #edb/ref 1061 3532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94008045-7999-599a-b725-36201f5023db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065198 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065198 #edb/ref 1061 3536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065198 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19a0572e-4edb-579e-b72d-fdc1fefe72ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065198 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065199 #edb/ref 1061 3537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d556d6dc-07a0-5db1-b5db-945a6a81b5eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065200 #edb/ref 1061 3539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065200 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7aabc08d-413a-5e15-8a0e-170869a0198c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065200 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065201 #edb/ref 1061 36121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "741a6139-aa26-54b7-8036-ade246aa12a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1061 4284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89060a03-576a-52b0-8345-c548845df17b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065240 #edb/ref 1061 4291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5eab9871-4247-51fe-85ce-5a836f6d3bc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-2)} & = \\\\ {a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have $a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a$ and $a^2 = a \\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\n a^3\n}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1065 1478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ce1b58b-8395-5986-99e6-cfd10bbca653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1064 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1065 1480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a2e18f4-6996-52a0-95eb-77abb10d0e77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1065 1481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40488864-6672-59b7-9ac6-79903856b484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1067 "The Power Rule With Fractional Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1064 "When simplifying an algebraic expression involving square roots, it's useful to remember that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|.\n$$\n\nIt's tempting to think that $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is equivalent to $x,$ but that's not true because $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is always non-negative, even if $x$ is negative.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $\\sqrt{x^2}$ for a couple of different values of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both cases, using absolute value gives the correct result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}=|4| & =4 & ✓ \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}=|-4| & =4 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1065 1604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e09a82d-1598-56ed-9888-9a1bfa380f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065439 #edb/ref 1064 "To expand an expression like   \n\n![](images/cd/cd1928f00c75236e8749153ea82e10efd3fc88d435eb095a940215337086e8a9.svg)  \n\nwe can use the distributive law. We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:  \n\n![](images/22/225564303e86ce4bce242c66c78434d94114de43c144933d6cadda2b718cfbc9.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065439 #edb/ref 1065 8871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065439 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3d4f07a3-afec-5d4a-a881-ea0271831a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065439 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to factor quadratic trinomials whose quadratic coefficient equals one. In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include cases where the quadratic coefficient is a positive integer that's different from one.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}x^2+{\\color{purple}{7}}x+{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the quadratic coefficient equals ${\\color{red}{2}}.$\n\nTo factor this trinomial, we must find two numbers that \n\n- *multiply* to give the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant $({\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6),$ and\n\n- *add* to give the linear coefficient $({\\color{purple}{7}})$.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:**  We start by writing down the factor pairs of $6{:}$\n\nThe factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6), \\qquad (2,3).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:**  Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{7}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+6 & =7 & ✓ \\\\ 2+3 & =5 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:**  Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=x+6x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:**  Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3 & = \\\\ x(2x+1)+3(2x+1) & = \\\\ (2x+1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! \n\nWe can check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+1)(x+3) & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot (x+3)+1\\cdot (x+3) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6x+x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1065 9455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7e6d3a2-936b-5775-9cf9-10135f0e6486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
